[[General election]]s were held in [[Bulgaria]] on 14 November 2021 to elect both the [[President of Bulgaria|president]] and the [[National Assembly (Bulgaria)|National Assembly]].<ref name="sofiaglobe.com">{{Cite web|date=11 September 2021|title=Bulgaria's President: Parliamentary and presidential elections will be '2 in 1' on November 14|url=https://sofiaglobe.com/2021/09/11/bulgarias-president-parliamentary-and-presidential-elections-will-be-2-in-1-on-november-14/|access-date=11 September 2021|website=The Sofia Globe|language=en-US|archive-date=11 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210911091511/https://sofiaglobe.com/2021/09/11/bulgarias-president-parliamentary-and-presidential-elections-will-be-2-in-1-on-november-14/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Bulgarians vote in elections for third time this year|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/11/14/bulgarians-vote-in-elections-for-third-time-this-year|access-date=14 November 2021|website=www.aljazeera.com|language=en|archive-date=14 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211114072115/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/11/14/bulgarians-vote-in-elections-for-third-time-this-year|url-status=live}}</ref> They were the country's third parliamentary elections in 2021, with no party able to form a government after the elections in [[April 2021 Bulgarian parliamentary election|April]] and [[July 2021 Bulgarian parliamentary election|July]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=11 September 2021|title=Crisis-hit Bulgaria to hold new snap election on 14 November|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/sep/11/crisis-hit-bulgaria-to-hold-new-snap-election-on-14-november|access-date=11 September 2021|website=The Guardian|language=en|archive-date=11 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210911115617/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/sep/11/crisis-hit-bulgaria-to-hold-new-snap-election-on-14-november|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=11 September 2021|title=Bulgaria to hold 3rd parliamentary election of 2021|url=https://www.theintelligencer.com/news/article/Bulgaria-to-hold-3rd-parliamentary-election-of-16451138.php|access-date=11 September 2021|website=The Edwardsville Intelligencer|language=en-US|archive-date=11 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210911143046/https://www.theintelligencer.com/news/article/Bulgaria-to-hold-3rd-parliamentary-election-of-16451138.php|url-status=live}}</ref> A second round of the presidential elections were held on 21 November 2021 as no candidate was able to receive a majority of the vote in the first round.<ref name="politico.eu">{{Cite web|date=2 September 2021|title=Bulgaria will hold November 14 presidential vote amid political crisis|url=https://www.politico.eu/article/bulgaria-presidential-election-politics-crisis/|access-date=2 September 2021|website=POLITICO|language=en-US|archive-date=2 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210902115308/https://www.politico.eu/article/bulgaria-presidential-election-politics-crisis/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="reuters.com">{{Cite web|date=2 September 2021|title=Bulgaria to hold presidential election on Nov. 14|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/bulgaria-hold-presidential-election-nov14-2021-09-02/|access-date=2 September 2021|website=Reuters|language=en|archive-date=2 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210902070909/https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/bulgaria-hold-presidential-election-nov14-2021-09-02/|url-status=live}}</ref>  

[[We Continue the Change]] won the most seats, although it was not a majority. Shortly after the election, they announced that coalition talks were going to be held.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Tsolova|first=Tsvetelia|date=16 November 2021|title=Bulgaria's centrist party launches coalition talks after surprise election win|language=en|work=Reuters|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/bulgarias-centrist-party-launches-coalition-talks-after-surprise-election-win-2021-11-16/|access-date=16 November 2021|archive-date=16 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211116112007/https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/bulgarias-centrist-party-launches-coalition-talks-after-surprise-election-win-2021-11-16/|url-status=live}}</ref> Incumbent president [[Rumen Radev]] gathered 66.72% of the vote, defeating university professor [[Anastas Gerdzhikov]] in a runoff.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Nikolov|first=Krassen|date=2021-11-22|title=Bulgarian President Radev elected for a second term|url=https://www.euractiv.com/section/politics/short_news/bulgarian-president-radev-elected-for-a-second-term/|access-date=2021-12-05|website=www.euractiv.com|language=en-GB|archive-date=5 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211205155502/https://www.euractiv.com/section/politics/short_news/bulgarian-president-radev-elected-for-a-second-term/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Nationwide turnout in the parliamentary and first presidential round fell to 40%, Bulgaria's lowest participation rate in 30 years for both presidential and legislative elections.<ref name="ec">{{Cite web|title=Electoral Commission of Bulgaria 2021 results|url=https://results.cik.bg/pvrns2021/tur1/rezultati/index.html|language=bg|access-date=15 November 2021|archive-date=14 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211114235946/https://results.cik.bg/pvrns2021/tur1/rezultati/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Nationwide turnout in the second presidential round experienced another drop, featuring only 35% of registered voters.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2021-11-25|title=ЦИК изчисли окончателно: 34.84% избирателна активност на балотажа за президент|url=https://news.bg/politics/tsik-izchisli-okonchatelno-34-84-izbiratelna-aktivnost-na-balotazha-za-prezident.html|access-date=2021-12-05|website=News.bg|language=bulgarian|archive-date=5 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211205155518/https://news.bg/politics/tsik-izchisli-okonchatelno-34-84-izbiratelna-aktivnost-na-balotazha-za-prezident.html|url-status=live}}</ref> 

The leaders of PP, BSP, ITN and DB announced on 10 December that they had agreed to form a coalition that would end a months-long political crisis. President Radev shortly after announced that he had given the mandate to form a government to Petkov.<ref name=":2" /> On 12 December, [[Kiril Petkov]] presented the composition of the [[Petkov Government|incoming government]], and it was approved on 13 December by the National Assembly.<ref name="ReferenceA">{{Cite web|date=2021-12-13|title=Bulgarian parliament backs Kiril Petkov as PM|url=https://www.politico.eu/article/bulgaria-parliament-kiril-petkov-prime-minister/|access-date=2021-12-13|website=POLITICO|language=en-US}}</ref>

==Background==
{{Politics of Bulgaria}}
{{See also|July 2021 Bulgarian parliamentary election|2016 Bulgarian presidential election|Bulgarian political crisis (2021–2026)}}
The [[July 2021 Bulgarian parliamentary election|previous parliamentary election]] which was held in July 2021, resulted in a narrow victory for the newly established [[There Is Such A People]] (ITN) over the ruling [[GERB]] party;<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|date=11 July 2021|title=Резултати парламентарни избори 11 юли 2021|url=https://results.cik.bg/pi2021_07/rezultati/index.html|url-status=live|access-date=14 November 2021|website=results.cik.bg|archive-date=4 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211104123408/https://results.cik.bg/pi2021_07/rezultati/index.html}}</ref> however, ITN won only 65 out of 240 seats in the [[National Assembly of Bulgaria]].<ref name=":1" /> Following the elections, ITN opted to try and form a [[minority government]] and started talks with potential partners ([[Democratic Bulgaria|DB]], [[Stand Up.BG! We are coming!|IBG-NI]], and [[BSP for Bulgaria|BSP]]) in order to secure their support. Nevertheless, these attempts proved unsuccessful, and ITN announced on 10 August that they were withdrawing their proposed cabinet, making a third election more likely.<ref name="rferl.org">{{Cite news|title=Bulgaria's Winning Party Withdraws Proposed Cabinet, Paving Way For New Vote|url=https://www.rferl.org/a/bulgaria-trifonov-new-elections/31402900.html|access-date=14 August 2021|newspaper=Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty|date=10 August 2021 |language=en|archive-date=14 August 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210814042704/https://www.rferl.org/a/bulgaria-trifonov-new-elections/31402900.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="balkaninsight.com">{{Cite web|date=10 August 2021|title=Bulgaria Edges Closer to Third Election in a Year|url=https://balkaninsight.com/2021/08/10/bulgaria-edges-closer-to-third-election-in-a-year/|access-date=14 August 2021|website=Balkan Insight|language=en-US|archive-date=13 August 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210813112810/https://balkaninsight.com/2021/08/10/bulgaria-edges-closer-to-third-election-in-a-year/|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Slavi Trifonov]], the leader of ITN, said in a video statement that this meant new elections.<ref name="rferl.org"/><ref name="balkaninsight.com"/> The mandate to form a cabinet went to GERB.<ref name="balkaninsight.com"/><ref>{{Cite web|title=Bulgaria's parl accepts withdrawal of populist TISP's PM-designate|url=http://seenews.com/news/bulgarias-parl-accepts-withdrawal-of-populist-tisps-pm-designate-750670|access-date=14 August 2021|website=seenews.com|language=en|archive-date=12 August 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210812143654/https://seenews.com/news/bulgarias-parl-accepts-withdrawal-of-populist-tisps-pm-designate-750670|url-status=live}}</ref>

GERB, the party of the previous prime minister [[Boyko Borisov]], said earlier that it would not try to form a government.<ref>{{Cite news|title=Bulgaria's Winning Party Withdraws Proposed Cabinet, Paving Way For New Vote|url=https://www.rferl.org/a/bulgaria-trifonov-new-elections/31402900.html|access-date=14 August 2021|website=Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty|date=10 August 2021 |language=en|archive-date=14 August 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210814042704/https://www.rferl.org/a/bulgaria-trifonov-new-elections/31402900.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The BSP said that if the scenario repeats itself, it would suggest that the incumbent caretaker cabinet becomes permanent. IBG-NI also expressed confidence that it could come up with a solution if handed the mandate to form a government.<ref name="balkaninsight.com"/> Trifonov subsequently announced that he would not support any other parties proposing a cabinet.<ref>{{Cite web|date=10 August 2021|title=Bulgaria could be heading for third general election in a year|url=https://www.euronews.com/2021/08/10/bulgaria-could-be-heading-for-third-general-election-in-a-year|access-date=14 August 2021|website=euronews|language=en|archive-date=14 August 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210814222107/https://www.euronews.com/2021/08/10/bulgaria-could-be-heading-for-third-general-election-in-a-year|url-status=live}}</ref> Parliament announced on 2 September that Bulgaria would hold the first round of the [[presidential election]] on 14 November, with a [[snap election]] likely to take place in the same month.<ref name="reuters.com"/><ref name="politico.eu"/>

On 6 September 2021, BSP handed back the last mandate of forming a government, meaning the parliament would be dissolved and a third [[parliamentary election]] would officially take place in 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2 September 2021|title=Bulgaria faces fresh elections as Socialists refuse to form a government|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/bulgaria-faces-fresh-elections-socialists-refuse-form-government-2021-09-02/|access-date=6 September 2021|website=Reuters|language=en|archive-date=5 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210905234845/https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/bulgaria-faces-fresh-elections-socialists-refuse-form-government-2021-09-02/|url-status=live}}</ref> President [[Rumen Radev]] declared on 11 September that there would be two-in-one elections on 14 November for the first time in Bulgarian history, where voters would be able to vote on the president and the parliament. This decision was taken "to save treasury costs and voters' time".<ref name="sofiaglobe.com"/>

==Electoral system==
The 240 members of the National Assembly were elected by [[open list]] [[proportional representation]] from 31 multi-member constituencies ranging in size from 4 to 16 seats. The electoral threshold is 4% for parties, with seats allocated according to the [[largest remainder method]].<ref>[https://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/3595/ Bulgaria] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210504022432/https://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/3595/ |date=4 May 2021 }} IFES</ref><ref>[https://www.idea.int/answer/ans1303551645461599 Electoral system for national legislature – Bulgaria] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210707030119/https://www.idea.int/answer/ans1303551645461599 |date=7 July 2021 }} IDEA</ref>

The [[President of Bulgaria]] is elected using the [[two-round system]],<ref>[http://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2207/ Bulgaria] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210911140445/https://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2207/ |date=11 September 2021 }} IFES</ref> with voters also given a [[none of the above]] option.<ref>[http://sofiaglobe.com/2016/10/09/bulgarias-2016-presidential-elections-voters-to-have-i-dont-support-anyone-option/ Bulgaria’s 2016 presidential elections: Voters to have ‘I don’t support anyone’ option] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211026212127/https://sofiaglobe.com/2016/10/09/bulgarias-2016-presidential-elections-voters-to-have-i-dont-support-anyone-option/ |date=26 October 2021 }} Sofia Glove, 9 October 2016</ref>

