==Campaign==
The campaign started after the election was officially announced for 14 November. The pandemic remained a large issue, especially with the rise of the [[SARS-CoV-2 Delta variant|Delta variant]] in the country.<ref>{{Cite web|date=8 September 2021|title=Bulgaria battles surge in COVID-19 cases amid vaccine opposition|url=https://www.euronews.com/2021/09/08/bulgaria-battles-surge-in-covid-19-cases-amid-vaccine-opposition|access-date=11 September 2021|website=euronews|language=en|archive-date=9 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210909110927/https://www.euronews.com/2021/09/08/bulgaria-battles-surge-in-covid-19-cases-amid-vaccine-opposition|url-status=live}}</ref> The anti-corruption parties ([[There Is Such a People|ITN]], [[Democratic Bulgaria|DB]], and [[Stand Up.BG! We are coming!|IBG-NI]]) were all looking to hold their ground, while the established parties ([[GERB]], [[Movement for Rights and Freedoms|DPS]], and [[Bulgarian Socialist Party|BSP]]) wanted to capitalise on public frustrations with the inability to form a government. The elections were widely hoped to bring an end to the political stalemate that had lasted since the [[April 2021 Bulgarian parliamentary election|April 2021 elections]], after which no government could be formed. This time, there was more pressure on the anti-establishment parties to start negotiating a coalition government after the elections.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Crowcroft|first=Orlando|date=12 November 2021|title=Bulgaria gets ready for its third election in eight months|url=https://www.euronews.com/2021/11/12/bulgaria-s-third-election-in-eight-months-brings-hopes-of-an-end-to-political-stalemate|access-date=13 November 2021|website=euronews|language=en|archive-date=12 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211112193039/https://www.euronews.com/2021/11/12/bulgaria-s-third-election-in-eight-months-brings-hopes-of-an-end-to-political-stalemate|url-status=live}}</ref>

After the president announced the new caretaker cabinet,<ref>{{Cite web|date=16 September 2021|title=Bulgarian President Radev names reshuffled caretaker Cabinet|url=https://sofiaglobe.com/2021/09/16/bulgarian-president-radev-names-reshuffled-caretaker-cabinet/|access-date=3 October 2021|website=The Sofia Globe|language=en-US|archive-date=5 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211005211249/https://sofiaglobe.com/2021/09/16/bulgarian-president-radev-names-reshuffled-caretaker-cabinet/|url-status=live}}</ref> the economy and finance ministers who were replaced in the reshuffle, [[Kiril Petkov]] and [[Assen Vassilev]], announced that they would compete in the November election as part of a new coalition named [[We Continue the Change]],<ref>{{Cite web|title=Former caretaker ministers Kiril Petkov and Assen Vassilev to announce their political project today|url=https://bnr.bg/en/post/101528674|access-date=3 October 2021|website=bnr.bg|language=en|archive-date=3 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211003214507/https://bnr.bg/en/post/101528674|url-status=live}}</ref> with the aim of becoming a new anti-corruption force that could form an effective government.

The global energy crisis was a large issue in the campaign, with [[natural gas]] prices soaring to record highs.<ref>{{Cite web|date=6 October 2021|title=Amid soaring prices, millions in Europe could struggle to heat homes|url=https://www.euronews.com/2021/10/06/amid-soaring-prices-millions-in-europe-could-struggle-to-heat-their-homes|access-date=10 October 2021|website=euronews|language=en|archive-date=10 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211010091330/https://www.euronews.com/2021/10/06/amid-soaring-prices-millions-in-europe-could-struggle-to-heat-their-homes|url-status=live}}</ref> Campaigning parties offered varying solutions to address this, with some supporting more reliance on Russia for energy, while others proposed [[Nuclear power in Bulgaria|domestic nuclear power]] in the longer term. The campaign was also influenced by the high levels of [[inflation]] in the country, which hit record levels at 4.8% in September,<ref>{{Cite web|date=19 October 2021|title=Bulgaria's inflation reaches nine-year peak in September|url=https://www.intellinews.com/bulgaria-s-inflation-reaches-nine-year-peak-in-september-224099/|access-date=23 October 2021|website=www.intellinews.com|language=en|archive-date=23 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211023124947/https://www.intellinews.com/bulgaria-s-inflation-reaches-nine-year-peak-in-september-224099/|url-status=live}}</ref> causing public dissatisfaction.

Another important issue was [[COVID-19 vaccination in Bulgaria|COVID-19 vaccinations]]. Due to a low vaccine take-up and high rises in the number of cases, the caretaker government implemented a "green pass", also known as the [[Vaccine passports during the COVID-19 pandemic|vaccine passport]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=19 October 2021|title=Bulgaria launches COVID-19 pass amid rising infections|url=https://www.euronews.com/2021/10/19/bulgaria-launches-covid-19-pass-amid-rising-infections-and-low-vaccine-uptake|access-date=23 October 2021|website=euronews|language=en|archive-date=23 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211023072245/https://www.euronews.com/2021/10/19/bulgaria-launches-covid-19-pass-amid-rising-infections-and-low-vaccine-uptake|url-status=live}}</ref> The green pass required citizens to provide proof of their vaccination status in several locations such as hospitals, schools and restaurants. The measure was met with widespread protests.<ref>{{Cite news|date=20 October 2021|title=Hundreds protest against Bulgaria's COVID health pass|language=en|work=Reuters|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/hundreds-protest-against-bulgarias-covid-health-pass-2021-10-20/|access-date=23 October 2021|archive-date=23 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211023124946/https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/hundreds-protest-against-bulgarias-covid-health-pass-2021-10-20/|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 6 October, the instructions of the Minister of Health and the Chief State Health Inspector for voting were approved, and did not differ from those for the elections on 11 July. Deputy Chairman of the Central Election Commission, Rositsa Mateva, said "there is no requirement for a green certificate for voting in the parliamentary and presidential elections on 14 November."<ref>{{Cite web|title=Bulgaria: The "Green Certificate" will Not be Required for the November 14 Elections - Novinite.com - Sofia News Agency|url=https://www.novinite.com/articles/211942/Bulgaria%3A+The+%E2%80%9CGreen+Certificate%E2%80%9D+will+Not+be+Required+for+the+November+14+Elections|access-date=5 November 2021|website=www.novinite.com|archive-date=5 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211105111350/https://www.novinite.com/articles/211942/Bulgaria:+The+%E2%80%9CGreen+Certificate%E2%80%9D+will+Not+be+Required+for+the+November+14+Elections|url-status=live}}</ref>

Bulgaria's Central Election Commission accepted the registration of 23 candidates for the presidential elections, announced after the deadline for applications on 12 October. This was the largest number of candidates in a presidential election in Bulgaria since the country began direct democratic elections for its head of state; previously, the highest number was 21, which occurred in 1992 and 2016.<ref>{{Cite web|date=13 October 2021|title=Bulgaria's 2021 elections: 23 presidential candidates|url=https://sofiaglobe.com/2021/10/13/bulgarias-2021-elections-23-presidential-candidates/|access-date=19 October 2021|website=The Sofia Globe|language=en-US|archive-date=13 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211013154758/https://sofiaglobe.com/2021/10/13/bulgarias-2021-elections-23-presidential-candidates/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The [[Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe]] (OSCE) announced in a statement that it would send an observation team to the general elections, following an invitation from the authorities of Bulgaria. It noted that the [[Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights]] (ODIHR) had previously observed 12 elections in the country, most recently the 11 July 2021 early parliamentary elections.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Presidential and Early Parliamentary Elections, 14 November 2021|url=https://www.osce.org/odihr/elections/bulgaria/502353|access-date=5 November 2021|website=[[Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe]]|language=en|archive-date=5 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211105111939/https://www.osce.org/odihr/elections/bulgaria/502353|url-status=live}}</ref>

